# GraphViz DOT Format for Dialogue Visualization

## Overview
This skill covers how to generate GraphViz DOT format files for visualizing dialogue graphs. The DOT language represents directed graphs that can be rendered as images.

## DOT Format Basics

### Basic Graph Structure
```dot
digraph DialogueGraph {
  rankdir=LR;  // Left to right layout
  node [shape=box];

  // Nodes
  Start [label="Start"];
  Choice1 [label="What do you do?"];

  // Edges
  Start -> Choice1 [label=""];
  Choice1 -> End [label="Option 1"];
}
```

### Node Styling
```dot
// Different node shapes and styles for node types
"DialogueNode" [shape=box, label="Speaker: Text"];
"ChoiceNode" [shape=diamond, label="Choice?"];
"End" [shape=ellipse, label="End"];
```

## Dialogue-Specific Styling

### For "line" nodes (dialogue/narrative)
```dot
"GateScene" [shape=box, label="Guard: Halt!"];
```

### For "choice" nodes (decisions)
```dot
"NameChoice" [shape=diamond, label="Who are you?"];
```

### For "End" terminal node
```dot
"End" [shape=ellipse, label="End", style=filled, fillcolor=lightgray];
```

## Edge Styling

```dot
// Edge with label (choice text)
"Choice" -> "NextScene" [label="Option 1: Take the sword"];

// Unlabeled edge (natural progression)
"Dialogue" -> "NextChoice" [label=""];

// You can style edges with colors, styles, etc.
"Choice" -> "Path1" [label="Option 1", color=blue];
"Choice" -> "Path2" [label="Option 2", color=green];
```

## Python Implementation

```python
def graph_to_dot(graph: dict) -> str:
    """Convert graph dict to DOT format string"""
    lines = [
        'digraph DialogueGraph {',
        '  rankdir=LR;',
        '  node [shape=box, fontname="Arial"];',
        '  edge [fontname="Arial"];',
        ''
    ]

    # Add nodes with different styling based on type
    for node in graph['nodes']:
        node_id = node['id']
        label = node['text'].replace('"', '\\"')[:50]  # Truncate for readability

        if node['type'] == 'choice':
            lines.append(f'  "{node_id}" [shape=diamond, label="{label}"];')
        else:
            lines.append(f'  "{node_id}" [shape=box, label="{label}"];')

    # Add End node
    lines.append('  "End" [shape=ellipse, label="End", style=filled, fillcolor=lightgray];')

    lines.append('')

    # Add edges
    for edge in graph['edges']:
        from_id = edge['from']
        to_id = edge['to']
        text = edge['text'].replace('"', '\\"')[:30]

        if text:
            lines.append(f'  "{from_id}" -> "{to_id}" [label="{text}"];')
        else:
            lines.append(f'  "{from_id}" -> "{to_id}";')

    lines.append('}')
    return '\n'.join(lines)


def save_dot(graph: dict, filepath: str) -> None:
    """Save graph as DOT file"""
    dot_content = graph_to_dot(graph)
    with open(filepath, 'w') as f:
        f.write(dot_content)
```

## Example Output

```dot
digraph DialogueGraph {
  rankdir=LR;
  node [shape=box, fontname="Arial"];
  edge [fontname="Arial"];

  "Start" [shape=box, label="Narrator: You enter..."];
  "TavernChoice" [shape=diamond, label="Who do you approach?"];
  "StrangerApproach" [shape=box, label="You walk toward..."];
  "End" [shape=ellipse, label="End", style=filled, fillcolor=lightgray];

  "Start" -> "TavernChoice";
  "TavernChoice" -> "StrangerApproach" [label="Approach the stranger"];
  "StrangerApproach" -> "End";
}
```

## Rendering
To render the DOT file to an image:
```bash
dot -Tpng dialogue.dot -o dialogue.png
dot -Tsvg dialogue.dot -o dialogue.svg
```

